// irgx — import topology of the Python binding.
//
// This is the floor the other three bindings stand on: the request model, the
// row decoder, the shell runner, the CFFI loader, the grade bands, and the
// error taxonomy. The three faces built on this library each ship a thin wheel
// that imports this one, so a change here is a change to all of them — which is
// most of the reason the layering below is worth writing down.
//
// Named `irgx`, not `irregex`, because that is the import name and because the
// Zig package next door already answers to `irregex` in `charter.zone`. One
// tree, two contracts, two names.
//
// Judged by `zoning verify` under the `python` dialect, from the repository
// root, alongside `charter.zone` (the Zig side).

package irgx {
    root     .
    language python
}

// Tiers, low to high. An import may only point back up the page.
zones {
    // How to compile the accelerator, and the C source it is about. Stdlib
    // only and below everything, because both of its readers run where nothing
    // is installed: the wheel hook inside a build backend, the build script in
    // a bare checkout. Its own zone rather than a corner of `packaging`, since
    // it is not a hook — it is the one answer to "which compiler, which flags,
    // which filename" that a hook and a script both ask.
    accel      accel/**

    // The wheel's build hooks. They run under hatchling and never under the
    // package, so they import nothing of ours and sit at the bottom.
    packaging  *.py

    // The generated contract surface: the row-schema table the engine and the
    // decoder agree on, and the calibrated grade bands. Depends on nothing
    // here, which is what makes it safe for everything else to depend on.
    contract   irgx/contract/**

    // The runtime and the request model it serves. One zone: the decoder
    // resolves an enum id to the request model's own calibrated type, and the
    // request model is decoded by the decoder — the two are a single unit of
    // change. See the cycle variance below.
    core       irgx/*.py irgx/runtime/**

    // Wheel-building scripts. Operational, above the package because they
    // drive it rather than the other way round.
    scripts    scripts/**

    // Behavioral tests, including the parity suites that hold the Python
    // decode path to the C ABI's own answer.
    tests      tests/**
}

// Reach outside the package.

// The in-process bridge to `libirgx`. Held by the core alone; the cold
// subprocess path answers without it.
use cffi by core

// Build- and test-time only, carried by the tier that runs under them.
use hatchling by packaging
use pytest by tests

// A sibling suite, reachable only because pytest puts the test directory on the
// path — which is why it reads as an outside module here and has to be written
// down. Two parity suites ask different questions of the same C header, one
// about its declarations and one about the shapes they reduce to, and both need
// it tokenized first. The alternative is a second copy of that tokenizer, which
// would let the two suites disagree about what the header says.
use test_cdef_header_parity by tests

// The same shape one tier down: `accel/toolchain.py` is reached by inserting
// its directory on `sys.path` and asking for `toolchain` by bare name, because
// neither reader can import the package it lives in — the hook runs before the
// package exists and the script runs in a checkout without it. So the edge
// reads as an outside module even though the file is ours, and it is written
// down rather than left to the `sys.path` line that makes it work.
use toolchain by packaging scripts

// The bench script borrows the suite's fixture that points `IRGX_LIB` at
// `zig-out`, so a measurement runs against the library this checkout just
// built. Same path insertion, same reason to name it: the alternative is a
// second copy of that discovery, free to disagree with the one the tests use.
use conftest by scripts

// The packaging test holds the wheel matrix to the script that builds it —
// `accel_shortfall` and `native_target` are the script's own verdicts, so the
// test asks them rather than keeping a second copy free to drift. Same
// `sys.path` shape as `toolchain` above, same reason to write it down.
use build_wheels by tests

// Structural laws.
limit  reach to 1 hop
forbid cycles across directories

// The one cycle, and why it stays.
//
// `request` is decoded by `runtime/decode`, so it imports it at load. The
// back-edge is `_typed()`, a cached lookup that maps an engine enum id onto
// the package's own calibrated Python type — so `row.grade.meets("strong")`
// works instead of handing back a bare string. Those types live in the modules
// that stand on the decoder, so the import is deferred to first use and the
// load-time graph stays acyclic.
//
// Breaking it would mean either giving up typed enums on decoded rows, or
// moving the type table into the decoder, where it would be a second copy of
// a contract the request model already owns.
variance cycle {
    irgx/request.py
    irgx/runtime/decode.py
} because "a decoded enum resolves to the request model's calibrated type rather than a bare string, and the lookup that does it is a cached deferred import so the load-time graph stays acyclic"
